meta {
  name: Mark asset ready with nothing uploaded
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/library/assets/{{assetId}}/ready
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-School-Id: {{schoolCode}}
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 409
  res.body.code: eq asset_object_missing
}

docs {
  This is the second half of the upload, and it fails here on purpose.

  Bruno cannot perform the signed PUT from request 2, so no object exists.
  Rather than trust the caller's word that the upload happened, the API HEADs
  the object and refuses: 409 asset_object_missing, "nothing has been uploaded
  for this asset yet". Read the assert as the documented outcome of skipping
  the upload, not as a bug.

  Upload the bytes first and the same request returns 200 with status ready.
  Three other refusals live on this path, each with its own code, and all of
  them are the store disagreeing with what the asset row claims:

  asset_size_mismatch     the object is not size_bytes long
  asset_digest_mismatch   the object does not carry the recorded sha256
  asset_digest_unavailable  the bucket has sha256 checksums turned off, so the
                          bytes cannot be verified at all -- a 503, because
                          that is an operator's problem and not the caller's

  Calling this twice on an asset that is already ready is a 200, not a
  conflict. Calling it on a deleted asset is 409 asset_deleted.

  With no object store configured for the school there is no HEAD to make and
  no asset to make it against: this answers 503 rather than 409, because
  request 2 never created a row. See the folder docs.
}
